          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Zeno of Elea</strong><br>\nZeno of Elea was an ancient Greek philosopher born around 490 BCE. He is famous for his paradoxes that challenged the notion of plurality and motion. Zeno was a student of Parmenides, who argued that reality is one, change is impossible, and existence is timeless and uniform. Zeno's paradoxes were created to defend these ideas and challenge the common perceptions of the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Life and Influence</strong><br>\nLittle is known about Zeno's early life, but his influence on philosophy is well-documented. He was said to have been from Elea, a Greek city in southern Italy, and his teachings are considered part of the Eleatic school of philosophy. His work was primarily focused on supporting his teacher Parmenides' philosophies, which were countercultural in that they went against the beliefs of many other philosophers of his day.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zeno's Paradoxes</strong><br>\nZeno is best known for his paradoxes, which were intellectual arguments that presented situations with seemingly impossible outcomes. The most famous of these is the 'Achilles and the Tortoise' paradox, where he argued that swift Achilles could never catch a slow-moving tortoise if the tortoise had a slight head start. This paradox challenges the conception of space and time in motion.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
