          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Purpose of the Paradoxes</strong><br>\nThe purpose of Zeno's paradoxes was to prove that the sensory evidence of motion and change could not be trusted, and that logical argument could reveal inconsistencies in the common understanding of the world. By presenting these paradoxes, Zeno aimed to show the weakness in trusting empirical evidence over reasoned logic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Mathematics and Philosophy</strong><br>\nZeno's work had a significant impact on both mathematics and philosophy. His paradoxes highlighted the need for a better understanding of infinity and the continuous nature of time and space. They have been discussed and analyzed by many philosophers and mathematicians throughout history and remain subjects of study and intrigue to this day.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Method of Argumentation</strong><br>\nZeno used a method of argumentation called reductio ad absurdum, which means reducing an argument to absurdity. He would take his opponent's argument to its logical conclusion, which would often result in an absurd or contradictory statement, thereby disproving the argument.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
